1
00:00:01,333 --> 00:00:03,566
After work you detour to supermarket pickup

2
00:00:03,833 --> 00:00:07,400
Same bag of laundry pods 15 yuan cheaper than home delivery

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:12,500
You ride more than 20 minutes line up at the locker then carry the bags upstairs

4
00:00:12,733 --> 00:00:14,933
When you get home the soup has gone cold

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:20,333
You meant to video call your mom but only replied maybe tomorrow

6
00:00:20,633 --> 00:00:25,333
You log the 15 yuan you saved and feel a little proud

7
00:00:25,533 --> 00:00:28,000
But this ledger leaves out those 40 minutes

8
00:00:28,166 --> 00:00:31,533
Nor the hot meal or the call you never made

9
00:00:31,966 --> 00:00:34,166
Most people misunderstand opportunity cost

10
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:36,433
It's not the money you already spent

11
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:40,633
It's the best alternative you quietly gave up to make that choice

12
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:44,000
We easily treat price as the full cost of a thing

13
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,200
Tagged at 49 so worth 49

14
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,233
Save 15 on a deal and you feel 15 richer

15
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,033
Because price is easy to see

16
00:00:50,366 --> 00:00:55,133
It's printed on the tag sits on the checkout page and leaves your balance instantly

17
00:00:55,533 --> 00:00:59,700
But what we regret most is rarely the money we can see

18
00:01:00,033 --> 00:01:02,233
It's what got moved aside for it

19
00:01:02,533 --> 00:01:06,500
You add one more thing you don't need just to hit the free shipping threshold

20
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:09,333
You think the extra cost is 20 odd yuan

21
00:01:09,533 --> 00:01:15,966
Later the closet holds one more shirt with tags on and your wallet keeps a little less slack

22
00:01:16,366 --> 00:01:20,533
You take a weekend gig the pay looks decent

23
00:01:20,633 --> 00:01:24,500
But after those two days you're still half asleep on Monday morning

24
00:01:24,933 --> 00:01:27,500
You think you just postponed some rest

25
00:01:27,733 --> 00:01:33,500
Only later you realize you gave up an afternoon to prep for interviews and a steady week ahead

26
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:36,933
You open a livestream the host says last 10 minutes

27
00:01:37,166 --> 00:01:44,000
You meant to stop for 2 minutes but between grabbing coupons and comparing prices over an hour slips away

28
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:45,500
You paid no entry fee

29
00:01:45,766 --> 00:01:53,366
But the unread pages the undone laundry the quiet sit you skipped all went with that hour

30
00:01:53,533 --> 00:01:56,966
This sounds like putting a price tag on every minute

31
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:58,200
Actually no

32
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:02,133
Opportunity cost isn't asking you to turn life into an abacus

33
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:06,333
It just reminds you that no choice happens alone

34
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:10,700
Every yes you say usually comes with an unspoken no beside it

35
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:14,000
That no is the real weight of many decisions

36
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:16,100
Especially when resources are tight

37
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:21,700
You hold a little money a little free time a little energy work hasn't burned through yet

38
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,133
They aren't cart items you can pile up and browse at leisure

39
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:31,000
Once given to one thing they can't go to another at the same time

40
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:35,733
Scarcity tells us you can't take everything you want

41
00:02:36,133 --> 00:02:38,366
Opportunity cost goes one step further

42
00:02:38,566 --> 00:02:41,800
It asks whether the thing you set down was worth it

43
00:02:43,133 --> 00:02:46,633
Economics calls this forgone value opportunity cost

44
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:48,333
The name sounds a bit stiff

45
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:54,533
In plain words it's the other best road you gave up to walk this one

46
00:02:54,633 --> 00:02:56,766
Note it's the single best one

47
00:02:57,366 --> 00:02:59,833
Not the sum of everything you didn't do

48
00:03:00,333 --> 00:03:08,533
Otherwise every meal would mean giving up the gym English side jobs family time and a movie all at once

49
00:03:08,833 --> 00:03:10,833
Count that way and no one could live

50
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:13,033
Opportunity cost asks one thing

51
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:18,300
Among the options actually on the table which one hurts most to give up

52
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:21,033
Xiao Lin has three plans Saturday afternoon

53
00:03:21,233 --> 00:03:25,700
Her company asks her last minute to cover a half day for 300 yuan in overtime

54
00:03:26,033 --> 00:03:28,733
A friend invites her to an exhibition ticket in hand

55
00:03:28,966 --> 00:03:32,400
She also promised herself to finish the portfolio two weeks overdue

56
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:34,366
She picks the overtime

57
00:03:34,733 --> 00:03:37,033
The 300 yuan is the gain she can see

58
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:41,300
But its opportunity cost isn't the exhibition plus the portfolio combined

59
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:46,600
She can't be in two places or stack two afternoons into one

60
00:03:46,966 --> 00:03:51,966
What she really compares is which of the other two matters more to her

61
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:57,500
If she's sending out resumes next week the portfolio may be the best alternative

62
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:01,566
Then what the overtime truly trades away isn't just half a day of rest

63
00:04:01,833 --> 00:04:05,200
It's a chance to move closer to her next job

64
00:04:05,633 --> 00:04:10,233
If she's been running on fumes for weeks sleep and energy nearly gone

65
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:14,500
Then the best alternative might be neither the exhibition nor pushing harder

66
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:16,800
But one uninterrupted afternoon

67
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:19,500
At that point rest isn't laziness

68
00:04:19,933 --> 00:04:22,400
It's the resource most worth holding on to

69
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:24,533
Same 300 yuan overtime pay

70
00:04:24,633 --> 00:04:27,533
For someone with rent due it may be worth it

71
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,333
For someone with a big interview tomorrow and no prep done it may not be

72
00:04:32,733 --> 00:04:35,133
Opportunity cost has no fixed price list

73
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:38,033
It depends on the resources you truly have

74
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:40,800
And on what you most want to protect right now

75
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:44,500
So it isn't telling you to always pick the highest pay

76
00:04:44,766 --> 00:04:49,966
Or to convert family time sleep and walks into hourly wages

77
00:04:50,433 --> 00:04:53,233
It just says don't stare only at the receipt

78
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:57,766
And to ask which road you turned away from for that receipt

79
00:04:58,133 --> 00:04:59,966
Some choices carry a tiny price tag

80
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,566
Yet they take up your most complete block of time

81
00:05:02,766 --> 00:05:04,966
Some choices seem to earn nothing

82
00:05:05,033 --> 00:05:08,533
Yet they save you a mind that can still think straight the next day

83
00:05:08,833 --> 00:05:10,966
The real bill isn't only at checkout

84
00:05:11,133 --> 00:05:13,766
It's also written in what never happened

85
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:19,400
Opportunity cost is hard to see because the price in front of you is clear

86
00:05:19,733 --> 00:05:21,433
That 15 yuan is easy to count

87
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:23,633
So is the 300 yuan overtime

88
00:05:23,733 --> 00:05:27,500
Even a membership coupon's savings pop up on screen instantly

89
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:31,600
But the thing you didn't do sends no receipt and no reminder

90
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:35,300
It only slowly takes shape when you look back

91
00:05:35,566 --> 00:05:38,833
What trips up our math is rarely the expensive stuff

92
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:43,400
It's the things that look cheap convenient or even free

93
00:05:43,966 --> 00:05:47,233
Type one valuing small money over whole hours

94
00:05:47,566 --> 00:05:50,500
You travel far to save the difference on two bubble teas

95
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:53,133
And burn half an hour scrolling on the way

96
00:05:53,600 --> 00:05:55,000
The money was saved sure

97
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,433
But the time that could have gone to tidying cooking or sleeping early got shredded

98
00:06:00,833 --> 00:06:02,933
Shredded time is the easiest to dismiss

99
00:06:03,166 --> 00:06:06,566
Unlike your bank balance it never warns you it's running low

100
00:06:06,933 --> 00:06:12,766
But once an evening breaks into a dozen five minute pieces deep focus is nearly gone

101
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,766
What you get isn't just cheaper bubble tea

102
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:19,533
It may be a tired night with nothing finished

103
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,300
Type two mistaking any income for a good deal

104
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:25,900
Some people accept every extra task

105
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:28,300
The reason is simple there's money in it

106
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:31,566
But tasks take not just your time but your attention

107
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:35,833
You reply to a dozen messages after work and fix a spreadsheet on the weekend

108
00:06:36,033 --> 00:06:37,833
Each one alone looks small

109
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:42,900
Together they squeeze out the energy for learning exercise and new chances

110
00:06:43,133 --> 00:06:44,500
You do want to get better

111
00:06:44,833 --> 00:06:50,233
Your clearest hours have been bought up by more urgent tasks that may not matter more

112
00:06:50,566 --> 00:06:53,800
Sometimes turning down small money isn't missing income

113
00:06:54,533 --> 00:06:57,400
It's keeping space free for big choices

114
00:06:57,833 --> 00:07:00,766
Type three mistaking free for costless

115
00:07:01,033 --> 00:07:04,966
You download a new productivity tool hoping it saves time

116
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:10,966
Then you spend evenings on templates settings note migration and comparing two more tools

117
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:15,000
Three days later you still haven't started the proposal you meant to write

118
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:17,166
The tool itself may be free

119
00:07:17,333 --> 00:07:19,400
But it demands your attention up front

120
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:22,900
Once attention is handed over it takes your momentum with it

121
00:07:23,133 --> 00:07:28,366
That's why more apps sometimes make it harder to start anything

122
00:07:28,700 --> 00:07:29,900
He doesn't lack tools

123
00:07:30,166 --> 00:07:34,433
He traded his best hours for the comfort of shopping for tools

124
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:38,533
Type four mistaking today's discount for tomorrow's good decision

125
00:07:38,733 --> 00:07:43,000
You see a limited time annual card originally 1200 now just 690

126
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:46,133
Afraid the price bounces back tomorrow you pay at once

127
00:07:46,633 --> 00:07:48,333
But you rarely go anyway

128
00:07:48,433 --> 00:07:57,000
What those 690 really compete with might be next month's checkup a dinner when your parents visit or the cushion you wanted to keep

129
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:59,766
The discount keeps you staring at the 530 saved

130
00:08:00,033 --> 00:08:01,833
It forces one more question

131
00:08:01,833 --> 00:08:05,500
Without that original price would I still pay this much for it

132
00:08:05,766 --> 00:08:07,033
That question is powerful

133
00:08:07,366 --> 00:08:11,800
It pulls you out of the seller's comparison and back into your own

134
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:13,766
Stop comparing to the list price

135
00:08:13,766 --> 00:08:16,100
And compare with your own next possibility

136
00:08:16,366 --> 00:08:20,100
This isn't about never buying never taking gigs never trying new tools

137
00:08:20,300 --> 00:08:25,100
You can still pick up in person take the overtime buy the annual card

138
00:08:25,300 --> 00:08:28,166
As long as you know what you're actually trading

139
00:08:28,333 --> 00:08:31,766
Seeing the trade and still choosing is a decision

140
00:08:32,100 --> 00:08:35,366
Getting pushed into a choice blind is what turns into regret

141
00:08:36,900 --> 00:08:40,933
The most useful part of opportunity cost isn't a complicated spreadsheet

142
00:08:41,133 --> 00:08:43,300
It hands you a tiny pause

143
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:48,800
Next time you're set to say yes to pay or to detour for a small saving

144
00:08:48,933 --> 00:08:50,833
Don't rush to ask is it worth it

145
00:08:51,033 --> 00:08:51,833
Ask first

146
00:08:51,833 --> 00:08:53,933
What am I setting down for this

147
00:08:54,166 --> 00:08:55,966
The answer shouldn't be a long list

148
00:08:56,300 --> 00:08:59,633
Just find the realest most worthwhile alternative right now

149
00:08:59,966 --> 00:09:04,600
About to spend more money ask what that money could have solved instead

150
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:10,966
About to spend more time ask who or what this hour was really meant for

151
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:17,933
About to take extra work ask can I still keep my sharpest self for tomorrow

152
00:09:18,166 --> 00:09:24,800
About to open another free tool ask will it crowd out the very step I need to finish

153
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:27,733
These four questions aren't meant to make you suffer

154
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:30,233
They make you choose more honestly

155
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:35,400
A lot of anxiety comes from having paid but not knowing what we paid for

156
00:09:35,633 --> 00:09:38,366
A little less money a little less time

157
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:43,433
We only see the first so life feels hollowed out for no reason

158
00:09:43,900 --> 00:09:47,700
Say your opportunity cost out loud and the choice gets clearer

159
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,766
You might still pay 64 yuan for home delivery

160
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:54,333
But now you know you're buying more than laundry pods

161
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:58,800
You're buying back 40 minutes and a hot meal you can sit down to

162
00:09:59,300 --> 00:10:02,166
You might still turn down that 300 yuan overtime

163
00:10:02,366 --> 00:10:04,633
This time not because you lack effort

164
00:10:04,766 --> 00:10:08,966
But because you know the portfolio or the sleep is worth more to you now

165
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:11,800
And you might still take the job

166
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:17,733
Once you see it trades away ordinary rest and what you need now is cash

167
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,500
It can still be a good decision

168
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:23,200
Opportunity cost never picks your answer

169
00:10:23,333 --> 00:10:26,500
It just stops you from treating price as the whole answer

170
00:10:26,800 --> 00:10:30,500
Scarcity says your resources can't be spent twice at once

171
00:10:30,733 --> 00:10:35,600
Opportunity cost says before you spend look at what it will crowd out

172
00:10:35,966 --> 00:10:40,366
You don't need to turn groceries and gatherings into financial reports

173
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:46,900
But before big choices that take money time energy and attention ten seconds of pause is worth it

174
00:10:47,366 --> 00:10:50,200
Name that unnamed alternative out loud

175
00:10:50,433 --> 00:10:54,500
Then decide if you're willing to trade what you have now for it

176
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:57,333
The costliest things never have a price tag

177
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:00,700
They just quietly vanish the moment you casually say yes

178
00:11:01,100 --> 00:11:03,200
Next time we cover marginal analysis

179
00:11:03,433 --> 00:11:07,600
Why the extra yuan and the first yuan are rarely the same thing
